<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<exercises xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ex_4_3">


<exercisegroup>
<title>Power Functions</title>
<introduction>
<p>
Determine whether a given function is a power function. If yes, identify the coefficient <m>k</m> and the exponent <m>p</m>. If not, say so.
</p>
</introduction>


<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
<m>y= \dfrac{0.8x^4}{0.2x}</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>y=4x^3</m>; <m>k=4</m>; <m>p=3</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle y=\frac{\sqrt{9x^8}}{x}</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>y=3x^3</m>; <m>k=3</m>; <m>p=3</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle f(x)=\frac{1}{3}\cdot 3^{x+1}</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Not a power function.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle g(x)=\sqrt{0.01x^6}</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>y=0.1x^3</m>; <m>k=0.1</m>; <m>p=3</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
<m>h(x)=0.01x^3-2x^2</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Not a power function.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle y=9\sqrt{\frac{x^5}{x}}</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>y=9x^2</m>; <m>k=9</m>; <m>p=2</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</exercisegroup>



<exercisegroup>
<title>Braking Distance</title>
<introduction>
<p>
<q>Braking distance</q> or <q>stopping distance</q> refers to the distance a car will travel from the point when its brakes are fully applied to when it comes to a complete stop
<fn><url href="http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/crstp.html">http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/crstp.html</url>, accessed: 7/5/20</fn>.
The braking distance is proportional to the square of the car's speed and it depends on the coefficient of friction, <m>\mu</m>, between the tires and the road surface. Let <m>D</m> denote distance, in feet, and <m>S</m> speed in mph. The formula for the braking distance is:
<md>
D=\frac{0.034}{\mu}S^2
</md>
Note that the braking distance does not include a driver's reaction time
<fn><url href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Braking_distance">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Braking_distance</url>, accessed: 7/5/20</fn>.
</p>
</introduction>


<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
Is the braking distance a power function of speed? If yes, give the coefficient <m>k</m> and the exponent <m>p</m>. Assume that <m>\mu</m> is a given constant.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Yes; <m>k=\frac{0.034}{\mu}</m>; <m>p=2</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
The coefficient of friction under normal conditions when the road is dry is <m>\mu=0.7</m>. What is the braking distance of a car that travels on a dry road at <m>35</m> mph? What is the braking distance at <m>70</m> mph?
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>59.5</m> feet; <m>238</m> feet
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
By what factor does the braking distance increase when speed <m>S</m> doubles?
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Factor of <m>4</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
The coefficient of friction on a wet road is <m>\mu=0.4</m>. Calculate the braking distance of a car traveling at <m>70</m> mph on a wet road.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>416.5</m> feet
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


</exercisegroup>



<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
Below are the graphs of four power functions <m>y=kx^p</m> where <m>p</m> is a positive integer. In each of the graphs, is the exponent <m>p</m> even or odd? Is the coefficient <m>k</m> positive or negative?
</p>

<sidebyside widths="24% 24% 24% 24%" margins="0% 0% " valigns="bottom bottom bottom bottom">

<image width="50%" xml:id="ex_4_3_1">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="ex_4_3_1_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\begin{axis}[grid=none,
xmin=-3,xmax=3,
ymin=-3,ymax=3,
xtick=\empty,
ytick=\empty,
minor tick={},
xlabel={}, ylabel={},
]
\addplot[domain=-2.5:2.5] {x^2};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>


<image width="50%" xml:id="ex_4_3_2">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="ex_4_3_2_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\begin{axis}[grid=none,
xmin=-3,xmax=3,
ymin=-3,ymax=3,
xtick=\empty,
ytick=\empty,
minor tick={},
xlabel={}, ylabel={},
]
\addplot[domain=-2.5:2.5] {x^3};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>


<image width="50%" xml:id="ex_4_3_3">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="ex_4_3_3_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\begin{axis}[grid=none,
xmin=-3,xmax=3,
ymin=-3,ymax=3,
xtick=\empty,
ytick=\empty,
minor tick={},
xlabel={}, ylabel={},
]
\addplot[domain=-2.5:2.5] {-x^3};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>


<image width="50%" xml:id="ex_4_3_4">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="ex_4_3_4_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\begin{axis}[grid=none,
xmin=-3,xmax=3,
ymin=-3,ymax=3,
xtick=\empty,
ytick=\empty,
minor tick={},
xlabel={}, ylabel={},
]
\addplot[domain=-2.5:2.5] {-x^2};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

</sidebyside>

</statement>

<solution>
<p>
From left to right:
</p>
<p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <m>p</m> is even and <m>k</m> is positive.
        </li>
        <li>
            <m>p</m> is odd and <m>k</m> is positive.
        </li>
        <li>
            <m>p</m> is odd and <m>k</m> is negative.
        </li>
        <li>
            <m>p</m> is even and <m>k</m> is negative.
        </li>
    </ul>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>




